 a maximum it's somewhat species  dependent but  um  about 300 microliters of blood so you're  not it's not a very large ask in terms  of the volume of blood that you would  need to go towards this so that's fairly  simple for you to get  um from numerous routes in in rat or  Mouse  the other component of this that I  didn't get a chance to address is you  can take these samples and you can  process them fresh if you're running  them right in your laboratory you're  going to collect them and then run them  the same day you can keep the samples  fresh and you can run them immediately  or within several days so they're good  for about five days if you want to keep  them refrigerated maybe get to them the  next day after your collection but we  also have a means for freezing samples  and this is really  um a great Advantage for those talk  studies because you know maybe you're  conducting this study for for numerous  other reasons you're getting  histopathology you're getting  um TK PK all those different other  components that you generally get as  toxicology studies and maybe one of  those is the contributing factor to to  you know the the dropping of that  particular compound you blew out the  liver or something like that if you  froze our samples away for say pig a um  mutational assessment you don't have to  run them right away you can store them  for months at a time and get back to  them in that way if it comes up that you  needed to look at a mutation endpoint  later on you don't have to start a whole  other dedicated study and invest in all  the time and the resources to do that  again you simply dip into the freezer  where you froze those samples away  and you can get that mutation endpoint  so there's several characteristics of  this assay that really drive it home  towards that 28 day toxicology study and  in fact that was really the study design  that we used for the development of the  essay so the bulk of the data that are  out there and that you read in